solovay-andrew@CS.YALE.EDU (Andrew Solovay) writes:
>It might be nice, in future, to have an 'appraise' command.  The usage
>would be:  While in a shop (perhaps only while next to the
>shopkeeper), you use the extended #appraise command, and specify an
>item; the shopkeeper then tells you what he would pay for that item.
 
cerebus@ucrmath.UUCP (edward tyrrill) writes:
>Why don't you just pick the item up.  The shop keeper will tell you how
>much it costs.  If it is too pricey for you then you don't have to keep
>the item.  Just drop it.  No need for extra options that duplicate things
>that can already be done in the game.

I think what Andrew was trying to say was that it would be nice
#appraise objects which are currently in your posession, not those
which are lying on the floor of the shop (for sale).

It would be nice, wouldn't it?  But I think that it my offset the
balance of the current game a little bit.  Those people who DO know
what the values of various objects will be quick to realize this as a
method to identify unknown objects.  Perhaps, then, we could use the
following (for 3.1):

As an extension of the #chat command, one could chat with the
shopkeeper (or anyone for that fact).  When you say '#chat', the games
comes back to say 'Chat about what? [-xxxxx*]', where '-' is "nothing 
in particular", the x's are objects, and of course '*' is a list of
objects.  Of course, some creatures will have a tendancy to lie or
just guess or just say nothing at all.   In other words, some
shopkeepers will try to lie so that they can buy your stuff at a
cheaper value, some will say it's worth a fortune, but won't buy it
for a fortune.  A nice 'side-effect' would be the ability to ask
Oracles information about certain items.  If the item were normal, the
oracle wouldn't care much about it.  But, if the item were named, then
the Oracle might be willing to tell you the capabilities associated
with your named weapon (or maybe just a legend...or maybe both,
depending on how much you pay him).  And then of course, if you were
to ask a friendly troll about this lock-pick that you have, it may not
say much at all.  Hobbits might be able to identify rings for you or
explain the capabilities of rings with all of the ringlore that they
know...

Actually, this would probably be a good feature as it may help some
novices learn about named weapons (from Oracles) and other such things
without having to help much help with it. 


